WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.170
- [Narrator] The Federal
Reserve might want

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:03.420
to fix your wallet

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.631
by turning it from this into this.

00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:06.464
(phone beeping)

00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:08.470
Though your wallet is still
designed for dollar bills,

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.390
Americans have been using cash
to buy things less and less

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.796
over the years.

00:00:12.796 --> 00:00:13.629
(till dinging)

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:16.010
That's part of why the Fed
is considering digitizing

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.820
the US dollar, giving
people money they can access

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.550
on their phone and bypassing
electronic payments

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.670
that can be slow and
costly for businesses.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.620
Some see this as a necessary upgrade

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.370
to the US financial
system but others worry

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.010
it could potentially
upend commercial banking.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.140
- There's some very, very
difficult questions to answer

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.970
but I think we, and we are
engaged in a serious program

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.060
to understand both the technology

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.584
and the policy issues.

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:45.720
- [Narrator] A central bank
digital currency, or CBDC,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.620
is exactly what it sounds like,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.240
a purely digital form of
a country's money issued

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.770
by its central bank that
people can use just like cash.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.920
It's true that cards
and apps already allow

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.300
for electronic payment

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.600
but behind the scenes,

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.230
these financial transactions
involve several steps,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.191
settling payments over
a patchwork of systems.

00:01:04.191 --> 00:01:06.350
- The plumbing of the system

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.820
is still based on a cash system.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.250
- [Narrator] Josh Lipsky is the director

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.660
of the Atlantic Council's
GeoEconomics Center

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.370
and says the US financial system

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.630
is still pretty old school

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.980
when it comes to moving money around.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.840
- It's based on banks talking to banks,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.220
saying hey, I need you
to transfer this to me.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.410
We've updated some of that over the years

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.040
but actually, if you were to look back,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.140
a lot of it dates back
to the 1950s and before.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.640
And that's not a great way to
run a modern global economy.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.040
- [Narrator] After a debit card is swiped,

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.110
the transaction can flow
through several entities,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.803
including point-of-sale systems,

00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:40.360
payment networks like Visa and Mastercard,

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.990
your bank and the merchant's bank.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:43.740
The system can take up to three days

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.360
to move money between accounts

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.230
and often comes with transaction fees

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.510
that can weigh heavily
on small businesses,

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.770
who experts say may have to pass the cost

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.910
onto customers or put
restrictions on card usage.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.070
These extra costs can limit their revenues

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.690
and customer base.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.250
CBDCs could bypass this system,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.020
allowing digital money to flow directly

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.300
between two people,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.200
in the same way you hand
over cash at the register.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.800
One method the Fed is considering

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.790
is issuing digital wallets
to Americans directly

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.100
or through commercial banks

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.640
or other financial service providers.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.060
The digital dollars themselves

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.040
would be no more than computer code,

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.340
possibly stored on a
central ledger at the Fed

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.780
or on a distributed ledger,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.510
like many cryptocurrencies.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.420
When you use your wallet
to pay for something,

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.770
the Fed would take the digital
cash out of your wallet

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.690
and deposit it into the merchant's,

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.060
simplifying the process

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.070
and doing it without fees.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.210
These could be done
through an app on a phone

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.900
or for those without smartphones,

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.680
an SMS text message or debit card.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.480
Besides frictionless payments,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.660
a digital dollar could help

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.840
the 8.4 million unbanked households

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.790
who don't have a debit or credit card

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.220
and can get left behind
in a cashless world.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.980
For example, instead of mailing checks,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.090
the government can deposit your tax refund

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.930
or stimulus payments directly
into your Fed wallet,

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.144
making the money available immediately.

00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:00.000
(till dinging)

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.700
Proponents also say CBDCs offer
a much more effective tool

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.550
for central banks to
enact monetary policy.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.700
Right now the Fed
influences interest rates

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.430
across the entire economy

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:11.700
by setting borrowing rates

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.760
between banks, known as
the Federal Funds Rate.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.620
But this is often a fraught process

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.650
that doesn't always produce
the desired results.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.290
- The Fed sets this benchmark rate

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.240
and other private banks
are supposed to cue off it

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.290
and then your mortgage is
supposed to cue off it.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.280
Does that really work?

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.880
How much time does it take?

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.610
And the answer that
economists will tell you

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.280
is it works but there're
probably more efficient ways

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.120
to transfer monetary policy.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.280
- [Narrator] Economists believe
that if the Federal Reserve

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.640
were to assign interest rates

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.860
to the money stored in digital wallets,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.030
it would be much more effective

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.040
at influencing economic decision making

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.530
at the household level.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.440
According to a January survey,

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.700
60% of central banks around the world

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.190
are currently experimenting with CBDCs.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.600
The Bahamas was the first country

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.290
to release a digital currency,

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.750
called the Sand Dollar in October 2020.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.200
And China has been developing

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.740
and testing a digital version of the yuan

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.260
but there are questions
about how a digital currency

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.203
would work in the US.

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:07.720
- Legitimate digital public money

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.575
could help drive out bogus
digital private money.

00:04:11.575 --> 00:04:14.320
It could help improve financial inclusion,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.600
efficiency and the safety
of our financial system

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.940
if that digital public
money is well designed

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.870
and efficiently executed,

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:27.670
which are two very big ifs.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.390
- [Narrator] The recent
rise of cryptocurrencies

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.160
like Bitcoin and private currencies

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.490
like Facebook's Diem

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.640
have spurred central banks

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.080
to explore CBDCs in an
effort to keep pace.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.950
Experts say these alternative
currencies expose the risk

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.930
of money and payments circulating
outside the watchful eyes

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.030
of central banks.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.540
- This is a blind spot for them

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.880
because when they're
setting monetary policy,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.510
they like to know how much
money is in circulation,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.860
broadly how that money is being used

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.720
and so that helps them
understand the macroeconomics

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.430
and the fundamentals of the economy

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.740
at any given time.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.480
- [Narrator] But by keeping
up with new competitors,

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.670
CBDCs could threaten to leave
traditional banking behind.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.660
- So if the Fed were to become a bank

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.240
in the traditional sense,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.710
and actually issue people money

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.810
and hold people's money directly,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.060
then you might hold less money

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.320
in your Bank of America app

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.850
and that would cut into
their business model.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.620
So they have to think about
how digital currencies

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:19.879
affect their business.

00:05:19.879 --> 00:05:21.400
- [Narrator] Because of these issues,

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.530
the Federal Reserve is
still studying the scope

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.910
and structure of how a
digital dollar would work

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.240
and how it could be integrated

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:28.840
into the current banking system.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.930
- The Fed doesn't wanna start dealing

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.210
with 300 million customers overnight.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.510
They're not equipped to do that.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.210
So you don't wanna call up the Fed

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.140
at midnight and say I forget my password.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.990
This is not something the Fed
wants to be doing or can do.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:43.920
So they need to think
about who to work with

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.530
to deliver digital dollars.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:46.620
- [Narrator] The Fed would also have

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.250
to tackle privacy concerns

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:51.070
over how anonymous digital
dollar payments should be

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.760
and whether the money should be restricted

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.170
to specific transactions

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:56.800
or programmed to expire
after a certain time period

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.260
to encourage spending.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.130
It plans to release a discussion paper

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.970
on the benefits and risks
of a dollar this summer

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.730
and has said it would move forward

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.610
without support from Congress.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:08.070
Many experts say the Fed will need

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:09.500
to figure out a digital dollar

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:10.630
to keep up with the pace

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.280
of a rapidly changing global economy.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.070
What that looks like remains to be seen

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:17.703
but it could mean using this a lot less.

